1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,625
Ears feel blocked or sore when a plane takes off or lands?

2
00:00:03,625 --> 00:00:05,370
Here's why, and how to fix it.

3
00:00:06,070 --> 00:00:08,151
Behind the eardrum is an air space.

4
00:00:08,151 --> 00:00:12,193
A tiny tube, the Eustachian tube, links it to the back of your nose.

5
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:15,950
When air pressure changes fast, the tube can't keep up.

6
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:18,287
The eardrum gets pushed and feels blocked.

7
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:20,664
1 — Swallow or yawn.

8
00:00:20,664 --> 00:00:23,913
Chewing gum or sucking a sweet helps open the tube.

9
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:29,703
2 — Or pinch your nose, close your mouth, and blow gently until your ears pop.

10
00:00:29,703 --> 00:00:30,629
Never force it.

11
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:32,801
Stay awake for landing.

12
00:00:32,801 --> 00:00:36,361
For babies, feed them or offer a pacifier so they swallow.

13
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:43,076
See a doctor if pain, muffled hearing or ringing lasts several days, or there's fluid or blood from the ear.
